<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Connecticut’s hurricane record</h2>
<p>The Great New England Hurricane of 1938 remains the benchmark storm for the state, flattening shoreline neighborhoods from Old Saybrook to Stonington. Hurricane Carol in 1954, Gloria in 1985 and Bob in 1991 followed. More recently, Tropical Storm Irene in August 2011 and Superstorm Sandy in October 2012 caused severe shoreline damage from Greenwich to East Haven, and Tropical Storm Isaias in August 2020 downed trees and power lines across nearly every town.</p>
<h2>Wind, water and the line between them</h2>
<p>Homeowners policies generally cover wind damage and exclude flood and storm surge, which are covered, if at all, under a separate flood policy such as the National Flood Insurance Program. On the shoreline, a single house can have both. Appraisal can value the wind portion of the loss, but the question of what caused each item of damage sometimes becomes a coverage issue.</p>
<ul>
<li>We document wind damage to roofs, siding, windows and openings separately from waterline damage.</li>
<li>We note interior water entry points so wind-driven rain is distinguished from rising water.</li>
<li>We coordinate with your flood adjuster’s scope so items are not double-counted or dropped.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Hurricane deductibles</h2>
<p>Many Connecticut policies, especially near Long Island Sound, include a hurricane or named-storm deductible expressed as a percentage of dwelling coverage. When it applies depends on the trigger written into the policy. Connecticut regulators have set rules on how these deductibles are disclosed and triggered, so read your declarations page and endorsement closely.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Check two policies</b>If you carry flood insurance, keep the two claims separate and file both promptly. Deadlines and proof-of-loss rules for federal flood policies are strict and differ from your homeowners policy.</div>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Does my homeowners policy cover storm surge?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Generally no. Surge and rising water are usually excluded and fall under flood insurance. Wind damage from the same storm is typically covered by the homeowners policy.</p></div></details><details><summary>When does a hurricane deductible apply in Connecticut?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It depends on the trigger language in your policy, which is often tied to an official hurricane warning or watch for Connecticut. Check your declarations and endorsement.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can appraisal resolve a wind-versus-flood dispute?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Appraisal values the amount of loss. Disputes over what caused damage can overlap with coverage, so some cases need an attorney’s view first.</p></div></details></div>
